The '''Ferrari F80''' (Type F250)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autoblog.com/features/ferrari-f250-prototype-caught-prowling-maranello|title=Ferrari F250 prototype caught prowling Maranello|website=Autoblog|first=Jonathon|last=Ramsey|date=26 July 2024|access-date=18 October 2024}}</ref> is a limited production [[Mid-engine design|mid-engine]], [[Hybrid vehicle|hybrid]] [[sports car]] built by the Italian automobile manufacturer [[Ferrari]]. Designed and named to commemorate the 80th anniversary of the company, it serves as Ferrari's flagship vehicle, and a successor to [[LaFerrari]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Ferrari F80 succeeds LaFerrari with 1184bhp F1 V6 powertrain |url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/ferrari-f80-succeeds-laferrari-1184bhp-f1-v6-powertrain |access-date=17 October 2024 |agency=autocar.co.uk |publisher=autocar.co.uk |date=17 October 2024}}</ref>

The Ferrari F80 was revealed on 17 October 2024, at an event with production started in 2025.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Barlow |first=Jason |title=Ferrari’s F80 Steals the Best F1 Tech, but It Should Have Been More Extreme |date=17 October 2024 |magazine=Wired |url=https://www.wired.com/story/ferraris-f80-steals-the-best-f1-tech-but-it-should-have-been-more-extreme/}}</ref>

== Design ==
The design of the F80 was inspired by the [[Ferrari F40|F40]], which commemorated Ferrari's 40th anniversary, and the [[Ferrari Daytona SP3]]. Certain styling elements, specifically the black band on the bonnet, were inspired by the [[Ferrari Daytona|Ferrari 365 GTB/4 Daytona]], similar to the [[Ferrari 12Cilindri]].<ref>{{cite news |title=The £3.1m Ferrari F80 is a V6 LaFerrari successor |url=https://www.evo.co.uk/ferrari/f80 |access-date=17 October 2024 |agency=evo.co.uk |publisher=evo.co.uk |date=17 October 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/car-culture/a61631354/ferrari-12cilindri-design-explained/|title=The Ferrari 12Cilindri Is a Retrofuturist Robot in a Daytona Mask|website=Road & Track|date=21 August 2024}}</ref>

== Specifications ==
=== Engine and transmission===
[[File:Ferrari F80.jpg|thumb|left|Ferrari F80 on display at the Ferrari factory in [[Maranello]] (in front of the Centro Stile)]]

The F80 uses a [[Hybrid electric vehicle|hybrid]] powertrain, consisting of the 3.0L [[Twin-turbo|twin-turbocharged]] ''[[Ferrari F163 engine|Tipo F163 CF]]'' 120° [[V6 engine|V6]] petrol engine derived from the [[Ferrari 499P]] [[Le Mans Hypercar]], and three electric motors. The engine produces {{cvt|900|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}}, while the electric motors produce {{cvt|300|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}}. The resultant [[specific output]] of the 2992cc engine individually is 296 hp/l, achieved in part via 3.7 bar (55.5 psi) of boost from the turbochargers. [[Motor Trend]] claimed both figures were the highest of any production car to date.<ref>{{cite news |date=10 July 2025 |title=The 2026 Ferrari F80's Technology Is Wild—Here's How It Works |url=https://www.motortrend.com/features/2026-ferrari-f80-technology-explainer |website=MotorTrend |first=Jonny |last=Lieberman}}</ref> Combined, the powerplants produce a total of {{cvt|1200|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}}.

=== Performance ===
The car has a stated top speed of 350 km/h (217 mph). It can accelerate from 0 to 100 km/h (0 to 62 mph) in 2.15 seconds and from 0 to 200 km/h (0 to 124 mph) in 5.75 seconds.<ref name="Ferrari" /> According to Ferrari, the vehicle generates over a ton of [[downforce]], producing 1,050 kg at speed. The car holds the fastest lap time for a road-legal Ferrari at the [[Fiorano Circuit|Fiorano]] test track, completing a lap in 1:15.30.<ref>{{cite news |date=17 October 2024 |title=Ferrari F80 is a 'Once in a Decade' Hypercar |url=https://www.goodwood.com/grr/road/news/ferrari-f80-is-a-once-in-a-decade-hypercar/ |website=Goodwood |first=Russell |last=Campbell}}</ref>

== Production ==
Ferrari stated that production began in 2025 and was expected to end in 2027. Only 799 units were to be built, all of which had already been reserved by customers, with each priced at approximately US$3.9 million (€3.6 million).{{Citation needed|date=October 2026}}

==Variants==
=== Rosso Supercar (2026) ===
On 24 April 2026, the Ferrari F80 was seen in [[Monaco]] with a [[Rosso corsa|Rosso Supercar]] livery, without the black stripe on the bonnet and without the black roof, unlike the version officially unveiled in 2025. This Ferrari is probably a tailor-made Ferrari and has been seen with a Monaco license plate number 711F. The car was collected by the owner from the official Ferrari dealer Scuderia Monte-Carlo.<ref>{{cite web |title=OFFICIAL FERRARI DEALER Scuderia Monte-Carlo |url=https://monaco.ferraridealers.com/en-GB |website=monaco.ferraridealers.com |publisher=Ferrari S.p.A |access-date=28 April 2026}}</ref>

